 "overview": {
  "summary": "Two independent circuits on one board, sharing only the digital control and the self-diagnostic multiplexer — §5-6 says so outright and troubleshoots them in two separate sub-sections. The CURRENT section makes the instrument's dc and ac output current, 20 µA to 220 mA in four ranges (220 µA, 2.2 mA, 22 mA, 220 mA), ac from 40 Hz to 10 kHz. It is a transconductance loop: the 22 V range of the DAC (A11) in dc or of the Oscillator Output (A13) in ac arrives on B IN/B FB, K1 and Q20–Q23 select it and its return, Z1's precision dual 10:1 divider compares it with the feedback, the dc amplifier on the heated hybrid HR2 amplifies the difference, Q2/Q3/Q18/Q19 turn that into complementary drive, and K2 sends the drive either to the 220 µA/2.2 mA amplifier (Q4, Q5, K3) or to the 22 mA/220 mA amplifier (Q6–Q13, K4). Four four-terminal shunts bonded to the same heated substrate — 10 kΩ, 1 kΩ, 100 Ω, 10 Ω — sense the current, K5–K9 select which, and buffers U2 and U3 close the loop. 22 V in and a 10:1 divider means 2.2 V full scale across whichever shunt is selected. K10–K15 and K17 switch the output, U4 buffers the guard, Q24/Q25 clamp compliance to ±11 V, and U5/U6 form an absolute-value monitor that reports current and compliance to the adc on A11. The 2.2 A range is not made here: A7 runs in its 22 mA range with K10 set, A15 multiplies by 100, and the current comes back on B CUR through K11. The HI-RES section makes a square wave from 10 Hz to 1.2 MHz with 4½-digit frequency resolution, which leaves on P LOCK HI and is what the Oscillator Output assembly (A13) phase-locks its oscillator to. A17's 8 MHz CLK/CLK* pair is squared up by comparator U13A and divided by four in U14 to 2 MHz REF; synthesiser U16 divides that by 2000 to 1 kHz and compares it against the VCO fed back through its own 6010-to-12000 divider; loop filter U20 drives varactor CR9 and the MC1648 VCO (U19) over 6 to 12 MHz; synthesiser U17 then divides by anything from 5 to 500 000, and U21 halves the result into the two output ranges that U18B/U18C select.",
  "sections": "§2-186 … §2-203",
  "connectors": "P211 and P212, DIN 41612 type C, right-angle, 64 pin each (Table 6-8, stock 807800). P211 carries the analog side — B IN, B FB, BRF13/BSRF13, OSC SENSE LO, R COM, DAC OUT LO, OS COM, AC CAL, B RCL/RCL, and the OUT LO / INT OUT HI / I OUT / I-GUARD output group. P212 carries the digital side — the guarded data and address bus, CS1*, CS7*, CS13*, WR*, RD*, RESET, POP, CLK/CLK*, RLY11*, P LOCK HI, and every supply the board uses.",
  "note": "A7 generates no supply of its own. +17 S, −17 S and S COM, +5 LH, −5 LH and LH COM, +5 RLH, +8 RLH and RLH COM all arrive on P212 from the Regulator/Guard Crossing assembly (A17). Only ±17 LH is made locally, by chokes L7 and L8 with C52 and C53 from ±17 S — which is why the hi-res half's supplies are referenced to LH COM while the current half's are referenced to S COM even though they come from the same regulators. Note that +8 RLH is a misleading name: §2-73 says it is the UNREGULATED +5 LHR from the Filter assembly fused at 3.15 A by F1 on A17, and fault code 3609 accepts +10 V to +20 V for it. It powers the HR2 heater through Q1 and nothing else on this board."
 },
